  "query_preview": "/MONDAY NIGHT THROUGH SATURDAY/...\nIssued at 141 AM CDT Sun Oct 18 2026\n\nTuesday and beyond are expected to feel quite pleasant for late \nOctober. Upper level ridging will remain over the central US with \nsome amplification midweek, which will further enhance its \nencroachment over the forecast area. Gradual warm advection along \nthe western flank of this ridge will provide a gradual but steady \nstream of increasing temperatures and seasonable moisture, as seen \nin the beginning of the week. Together, these will support daily \ntemperatures near 7-10+ degrees above normal. \n\nDry conditions are expected in conjunction with this warming \ntrend, which brings some concern for fire weather potential. \nHowever, there are two factors at play that should help limit \nthe potential: a gradual increase in low-level moisture and a \nbroad pressure gradient inducing weak surface winds. As such, \nwhile the threat appears low at this time, this remains an area \nto watch as we progress through the week.\n\nTowards the end of the week, a pattern change appears possible as a \nmidlevel trough plows into the ridge. This may be our next chance \nfor measurable precipitation, though a sizable amount of dry \nair to overcome keeps confidence low at this time.\n\nBeyond 7 days... \n\nRecurving typhoons in western Pacific often portend a \ncentral/eastern CONUS trough in about 6-14 days (buckling of jet \norientation over the north Pacific which can pump up the ridge into \nAlaska and the western CONUS in turning fostering an eastern \ntrough), and probably closer to 14 days in this case with Typhoon \nChampi recurving further west towards China. Something to monitor \nfor a pattern change to colder and potentially a period of \nunsettled/wet weather. Latest CPC 8-14 day outlooks Oct 25-31 lean \nabove normal on precipitation and likely above normal temperatures \nthough again toward the end of the period is when we may see the \nevolution toward more troughing and colder conditions.",
